<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treblinka extermination camp</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a>–<a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Treblinka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Treblinka was a Nazi extermination camp north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> in German-occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, built as part of Operation Reinhard. Between July 1942 and October 1943 some 700,000 to 900,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, most of them from the Warsaw Ghetto, were murdered there in gas chambers, more than at any camp except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a>. Prisoners staged a revolt in August 1943, and the Germans demolished the camp soon after.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treblinka-extermination-camp.jpg" alt="Treblinka extermination camp" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Little Savage · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treblinka_-_Rail_tracks.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
